      "threadSnapshot": {
        "title": "inlineFonts drops @font-face when font-family is set through a custom property",
        "body": "With `inlineFonts: true` (and `preloadFonts: true`), beasties keeps only the `@font-face` rules whose family appears in a critical `font-family` / `font` declaration. A family written through a custom property is never resolved, so its `@font-face` rules are dropped from the inlined CSS. Tailwind v4 always sets the base font this way (`font-family: var(--default-font-family, ...)` in its preflight), so a Tailwind v4 site loses all its `@font-face` rules.\n\nThis is a regression from #422, released in 0.5.4: 0.4.3 to 0.5.3 inline the rule in the reproduction below, 0.5.4 and 0.5.5 drop it, with the classic and the compiled paths alike. `main` still collects the families with `parseFontFamilies(decl.prop, decl.value)`.\n\nReproduction:\n\n```css\n@font-face { font-family: Gotham; src: url(/fonts/g.woff2) format(\"woff2\") }\n:root { --font-secondary: Gotham, sans-serif }\nbody { font-family: var(--font-secondary) }\n```\n\n```html\n<html><head><link rel=\"stylesheet\" href=\"styles.css\"></head><body><p>text</p></body></html>\n```\n\nExpected: the `@font-face` rule is inlined, as it is with `body { font-family: Gotham, sans-serif }`. Actual: it is dropped, because `parseFontFamilies('font-family', 'var(--font-secondary)')` returns `[\"var(--font-secondary)\"]`.\n\n#452 fixed the same case for `@keyframes` named through a custom property. Following `var()` the same way for `font-family` and `font` would cover this one.\n\nSeen through Angular 22.2 (`@angular/build` and `@angular/ssr` 22.2.x depend on beasties 0.5.4): in a headless Chrome holding only the inlined critical CSS, our server-rendered page loads Gotham with beasties 0.4.3 and no font at all with 0.5.4, so its text renders in the system fallback until the full stylesheet loads.\n",
